  "step_2": "The individual expresses skepticism about receiving any money at all, stating 'Da ich nicht der Meinung bin, in diesem Szenario überhaupt Geld zu erhalten...' (Since I don't believe I will receive money in this scenario at all...). The individual chooses the option with a 'verschwindend geringe Option, überhaupt plus zu machen' (vanishingly small chance to make a positive outcome), which suggests a preference for having any chance at a positive payoff. The justification centers on the psychological irrelevance of the decision due to the extremely low probabilities, yet a slight lean toward the possibility of a gain. The individual does not mention the concept of a 'sure outcome' or certainty, nor do they contrast the lotteries based on whether one has a guaranteed result. The focus is on the near-zero probability of gain and the emotional or cognitive perception of the choice as nearly meaningless, but still slightly favoring any chance of a positive outcome.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the presence or absence of a sure outcome (i.e., 100% probability outcome) in each lottery. However, the individual does not consider or refer to the concept of certainty or sure outcomes. Instead, the individual focuses on the minimal but non-zero chance of a positive gain, which aligns more with a 'possibility effect' or a preference for any chance of a gain under extreme uncertainty. There is no indication that the individual assessed whether either lottery had a sure outcome (which neither does), nor did they use the absence of a sure outcome as a basis for preference. The justification in the verbal report is based on the psychological weight of having any chance to win, not on structural features like certainty. Therefore, the information considered and the reasoning rule applied by the individual do not align with the decision reason. The reason cannot be said to have been used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
